Embedding threads from EMBEDDING_THREADS, and sub-batches of 32 texts in order of length

Threads. Unset, ONNX Runtime starts one intra-op thread per core and pins its
pool threads to cores 1..n-1 in every process (on 8 vCPUs: Cpus_allowed_list 1
to 7), so with P batch processes at once their pools share the same cores. In
the modama search stack on 8 vCPUs, 4 processes with the default embedded 118
texts/s at 62.6 ms CPU per text, 4 processes x 2 threads 194 texts/s at
36.4 ms, 8 x 1 206 texts/s. fastembed passes `threads` as
intra_op_num_threads, set explicitly, which also stops the pinning. Unset,
nothing changes.

Sub-batch. `embed` without `batch_size` takes 256 texts at a time; one batch
process then peaks at 5.3 GB RSS for 250 texts of 512 tokens and at about 9 GB
from 1,000 texts on (bge-small-en-v1.5); with 32 it peaks at 1.40 GB for 250 and
1.42 GB for 2,000, at about 7 % more time (48.0 against 45.0 s for 250 texts of
512 tokens). The bound lets a worker size its processes by memory.

Order of length. fastembed pads every sub-batch to its longest text, so a short
text in a sub-batch with one of 512 tokens costs as much as a long one. The
texts are embedded in order of their length in characters and the vectors are
returned in the given order. In the modama search stack's VM (8 vCPUs, one batch
process, 1 thread), a batch of 250 of the VM's records (19 to 512 tokens) took
18.4 instead of 130.2 s (7.1x; 85 against 532 ms CPU per record), at about 190
tokens 2.8x, at 512 tokens throughout the same; the bulk bodies of that batch in
the given order and sorted held the same 250 embeddings, bit for bit.

Against metadata-warehouse v1.10.0 with 0001 applied; applied with `patch -p1` in the image
build (modama search stack, warehouse-airflow.Dockerfile; change cpu-budget, D7, D9, H1).

